Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Corridor 6 Has No Exit
- Story & Context: During a rehearsal, Elise follows a maintenance sign marked Corridor 6 and discovers that the passage ends at a glass wall that had been added after the original plans were printed. Thabo records her footsteps approaching the dead end and uses the spacing between them as the basis for the kick pattern. Ana writes a three-note brass cell that stops one pitch before resolution, while Luka bows a damped vibraphone bar until its overtone begins to beat against the Ondes.

The first half of the track stays sparse: kick, tuba pedal, footsteps, and a single wavering Ondes line. The brass appears only after the listener has internalized the empty corridor. In the final section, every instrument except the field recording disappears, leaving the sound of Elise turning around and walking back the same distance.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long unfinished archive corridor ending unexpectedly at a translucent glass wall, maintenance sign reading Corridor 6, black cable on concrete floor, faint reflection of a brass bell, one cold overhead work light, documentary realism, desaturated gray-green palette. Spoken text limited to practical phrases such as "wrong plan," "turn back," and "same distance out."

Track 2: Three Horns Behind Frosted Glass
- Story & Context: Ana places the trumpet, tenor horn, and baritone player in separate rooms divided by frosted partitions. Because the musicians cannot see one another, they perform a short phrase using only the reflected Ondes tone as a timing cue. Each player enters a fraction late, producing a slow rolling chord that no score could reproduce exactly.

Thabo builds a sparse 116 BPM gqom skeleton beneath the delays but removes the kick whenever all three horns overlap. Luka strikes muted vibraphone bars with rubber mallets, then sends only their tape echoes back into the corridor. The central musical event is not a crescendo but one accidental moment when all three brass attacks align perfectly; immediately afterward, the arrangement returns to misalignment.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Three silhouettes holding brass instruments behind separate frosted glass partitions, trumpet valves and baritone tubing visible only as partial shapes, Ondes keyboard reflected in the glass, narrow utility lighting, realistic architectural photography. Vocal material should be minimal and procedural: "again from the breath," "do not watch," and "wait for the tone."

Track 3: Archive Box Full of Reverb
- Story & Context: Luka discovers a stack of empty archival boxes and places a small speaker inside one while testing a tape-delay loop. The cardboard cavity removes much of the high end and turns the returning brass phrase into a dull, distant pulse. Rather than treating the effect as a novelty, the band records the box as a physical filter and uses its resonance as the track's main midrange texture.

The arrangement begins with no melodic instrument at all: sub-bass, low kicks, box resonance, and ventilation noise. Elise enters with a narrow Ondes interval that bends slowly between two brass pitches. Marko answers with low tuba notes that are immediately muted, leaving only their box-filtered echoes. Near the end, the archive box collapses slightly under the speaker's weight, changing the resonance. The group keeps the damaged take and stops the track there.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Plain archival cardboard box on a concrete floor with a small speaker inside, tape echo unit beside it, one bent corner collapsing inward, tuba tubing and Ondes ribbon controller blurred in the background, muted beige and charcoal industrial realism. Spoken fragments limited to "leave the box," "record the fold," and "do not rebuild it."

